From a4dac745232cf30899fb4380a6b087a3d3c75460 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Szpakowski <michi.szpakowski@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 15:52:02 +0200
Subject: [RFC PATCH 2/2] ALSA: hda/cs8409: Add support for Apple
 MacBookPro13,1

The 2016 13" MacBook Pro without Touch Bar has a CS8409 with codec
subsystem ID 106b:3300, a CS42L83 for the headset jack and four SSM3515
speaker amplifiers, all on the I2C bridge of the CS8409. Add it as a new
board next to the Dell ones.

Layout: the speakers are on ASP1 (pins 0x24/0x25, converters 0x02/0x03),
the amplifiers at I2C 0x14-0x17, one TDM slot each. The CS42L83 is on
ASP2 (headphone pin 0x2c from converter 0x0a, microphone pin 0x3c to
ADC 0x1a). The internal microphone is DMIC1. GPIO 0 is the CS42L83
interrupt, GPIO 1 its reset.

The register values come from the out-of-tree snd_hda_macbookpro driver
(https://github.com/davidjo/snd_hda_macbookpro), which replays the
sequences of AppleHDA, read down to the register level and put into the
structure of this driver: the vendor coefficient table, the CS42L83 init
sequence with the clocking AppleHDA uses for 44.1 kHz, the SSM3515
setup. Only 44.1 kHz is supported, as nothing else is known.

What differs from the Dell boards:

- The fixup is also picked by DMI. The firmware writes the codec
  subsystem ID at boot and a link reset puts the CS8409's own ID back,
  so the ID is not there after the module has been reloaded.

- The CS42L83 serial port carries 44.1 kHz in a 48 kHz frame through
  the sample rate converters, on a PLL fed from the bit clock. Started
  once from the init, as the Dell setup does, the headphone path came up
  right for one stream and distorted or silent for the next. So the
  blocks are powered, the PLL started and the serial port channels
  enabled when a stream is prepared, in that order, and taken down when
  the last stream is cleaned up, as the ASoC cs42l42 driver and AppleHDA
  do. The PLL is also stopped before a system suspend powers the CS42L83
  down under a running stream, which removes a "Timeout waiting for
  PDN_DONE".

- Runtime suspend keeps the CS42L83 out of reset, powered down except
  for the detect block and on its oscillator, with the tip sense
  interrupt armed for the opposite of the present jack state and the
  GPIO unsolicited response left enabled. A plug or unplug then wakes
  the codec, and a headset that stays plugged is not detected again on
  every resume: that detection ran under each stream started from idle,
  with the speakers on until it was done. On the way out of standby the
  latched detect status says what woke the codec and the tip sense level
  is given time to follow it: after an unplug the debounced level reads
  "present" for about half a second more. The full init remains for the
  first init, after S3, where the CS8409 loses its registers, and after
  a system suspend.

- An interrupt raised before the card is registered is recovered. The
  HDA core drops unsolicited responses until then, the jack detection
  run from the build action raises its interrupt before, nobody reads
  the CS42L83 status and the line stays low: no later event makes an
  edge. A delayed work looks at the line once responses get through.

- The speakers get a "Speaker Playback Volume" on the digital volume of
  the amplifiers. It stops at the -3 dB AppleHDA programs, since nothing
  here knows what the drivers stand above it.

Tested on a MacBookPro13,1 with this driver built as a module against
Fedora's 7.2.8: speakers, speaker volume, headphones and headset
microphone (Koss Porta Pro with microphone), internal microphone, plug
and unplug with a stream running and with the codec runtime suspended,
playback started from idle, S3 from idle and under a running stream.

Not handled: headset buttons; sample rates other than 44.1 kHz. The
headset type detection, which is the cs42l42 code of this driver, took
the headset for headphones three times in about 30 plugs.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
